# --- s057 Book I d.19 q.2
n("n890","s057-01","vtrum vna persona sit in alia per circumincessionem","la","source","Corresponds to Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 19 q. 2 (on the mutual in-being, *circumincessio*, of the divine persons).","to-verify")
n("n891","s057-01","neque confundentes personas","la","intertext","*Symbolum Quicumque*, v. 4: *neque confundentes personas, neque substantiam separantes*.","certain")
n("n892","s057-01","Johan. 14. ego in patre et pater in me est","la","intertext","Jn 14:10 (*Ego in Patre et Pater in me est*).","certain")
n("n893","s057-02","opinio Henrici in summa","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Summa quaestionum ordinariarum*. The article and question are not specified by Gorris.","to-verify")
n("n894","s057-02","⟨ratione⟩","la","textual","A prints *tatione*, an evident misprint. *ratione* is conjectured; *in ratione distinctionis* perhaps means ‘in the argument for the distinction’, but the sense is not clear. B not compared.","conjecture")
n("n895","s057-02","Tangitur frater Guillermus varronis","la","intertext","‘Guillelmus Varro(nis)’ is the usual late-medieval name of William of Ware OFM, Scotus's presumed master. Gorris identifies Scotus's anonymous target as Ware's *Sentences* commentary, I d. 19, single question, third opinion. The reference has not been checked; Ware's commentary is largely unedited. This is a significant intertextual pointer.","to-verify")
n("n896","s057-03","libro de fide ad Petrum","la","intertext","*De fide ad Petrum* is by Fulgentius of Ruspe but was transmitted under Augustine's name. The passage quoted in ¶6 (*propter unitatem naturalem totus Pater in Filio et Spiritu sancto est …*) is *De fide* 1.4 (CCSL 91A), and it is cited by Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 19 c. 4.","verified")
n("n897","s057-03","Hylarium. 3° de trinitate","la","intertext","Hilary, *De Trin.* III 1–4 (on Jn 14:10).","to-verify")
n("n898","s057-03","Ambrosium in secunda epistola ad Corinth.","la","intertext","The Pauline commentary is probably that of ‘Ambrosiaster’, transmitted as Ambrose's and cited by Lombard in d. 19. The passage has not been identified.","to-verify")
n("n899","s057-04","philosophus. 5. phicorum. quod homo sanatur quia cor vel pectus sanatur","la","intertext","Aristotle, *Phys.* V 1, 224a21–30 (things changed *per partem*: the man is healed because his chest is healed). *phicorum* is A's abbreviation.","certain")
n("n900","s057-04","ethereogeneis","la","textual","Thus A, for *heterogeneis*. It is glossed in the text as *partium dissimilium*.","verified")
n("n901","s057-05","sicut auis est in laqueo per pedem","la","intertext","The bird held in the snare by its foot is a stock scholastic example of being-in *per partem*.","to-verify")
n("n902","s057-05","esset circumincessione in alio","la","textual","Thus A; the construction is defective (one would expect *circumincessio vnius in alio*). B not compared.","uncertain")
n("n903","s057-06","Ambrosium in hymno. totus in verbo pater et totus in patre filius","la","intertext","The hymn has not been identified. The same words are attributed to Ambrose in ¶5 without the word ‘hymn’. Lombard's d. 19 is the probable intermediate source.","to-verify")
n("n904","s057-07","Quamuis essentia sit principalis ratio","la","textual","A has the abbreviation *Q̄uis*, expanded here as *Quamuis* from the sense. *Quis* would be meaningless.","conjecture")
n("n905","s057-07","4°. phisicorum","la","intertext","Aristotle, *Phys.* IV 3, 210a14–24: the eight modes of being-in (*in aliquo esse*).","certain")
n("n906","s057-07","insistentie","la","textual","Thus A (*insistentie*), apparently for *inexistentie*, the word used in the next clause. It is kept.","uncertain")
n("n907","s057-02","William of Ware","en","translation","Latin *Guillermus varronis*.")
n("n908","s057-07","[if it be asked whether]","en","translation","Supplied: the Latin states the question elliptically.")

